<%= lato_page_head "Documentation" %>

<h2>How to use Lato Users</h2>

<p>
  <code>lato_users</code> adds a user management section to a Lato admin panel.
  Use it when the application needs admins to create users, edit user data, manage invitations,
  and handle account verification from the back office.
</p>

<h3>Installation</h3>

<p>Requires Rails 8.1.3.1 or newer in the 8.1 series and Lato 3.25.0 or newer.
Existing applications must run <code>rails lato:install:migrations</code> (Lato session records) and <code>rails db:migrate</code>
before deployment. Previous sessions are invalidated.</p>

<p>
  The host application also needs a configured Action Mailer (verification and invitation emails) and a working
  <code>Rails.cache</code> store (email semaphores and verification codes). Password parameters must be filtered
  from logs (<code>config.filter_parameters</code> including <code>:passw</code>).
</p>

<p>Add Lato Users to the application Gemfile:</p>

<pre><code>gem "lato"
gem "lato_users"</code></pre>

<p>Install the gem and its migration (adds the <code>lato_users_admin</code> column). <code>rails lato_users:install:application</code> is a no-op and is not required:</p>

<pre><code>bundle
rails lato_users:install:migrations
rails db:migrate</code></pre>

<p>Mount the engine in <code>config/routes.rb</code>:</p>

<pre><code>Rails.application.routes.draw do
  mount LatoUsers::Engine =&gt; "/lato-users"

  # ...
end</code></pre>

<p>Import styles in <code>app/assets/stylesheets/application.scss</code>:</p>

<pre><code>@import "lato_users/application";</code></pre>

<p>Import JavaScript in <code>app/javascript/application.js</code>:</p>

<pre><code>import "lato_users/application"</code></pre>

<p>Add the menu entry, visible only to admins (the engine activates <code>:lato_users</code> on its pages):</p>

<pre><code>&lt;% if @session.user&amp;.lato_users_admin %&gt;
  &lt;%= lato_navbar_nav_item :lato_users, lato_users.root_path do %&gt;Users&lt;% end %&gt;
&lt;% end %&gt;</code></pre>

<p>
  Routes under the mount path: <code>/</code> (redirects to users), <code>users</code>
  (with <code>resend_verification_email</code>, <code>manual_verify_email</code>,
  <code>revoke_email_verification</code>, <code>force_update_password</code>) and
  <code>invitations</code> (with <code>resend</code>).
</p>

<h3>Permissions</h3>

<p>
  Users must be logged into Lato and must have Lato Users admin permission enabled.
  Grant permission from application seeds or from an existing admin account.
</p>

<pre><code>user = Lato::User.find_by(email: "admin@example.com")
user.update!(lato_users_admin: true)</code></pre>

<div class="alert alert-warning">
  <p class="mb-0">
    <code>lato_users_admin</code> is effectively a super-admin permission: it can grant any other engine's
    admin permission (<code>lato_settings_admin</code>, <code>lato_spaces_admin</code>, <code>lato_storage_admin</code>,
    <code>lato_cms_admin_role</code>, ...) to any user. Admins cannot remove their own <code>lato_users_admin</code>,
    and the last users admin can be neither demoted nor deleted. Non-admin requests are redirected to the Lato root
    (<code>403</code> for non-HTML formats).
  </p>
</div>

<h3>Admin permissions in the user form</h3>

<p>
  The user form loads the available admin permissions automatically. Each Lato engine
  (for example <code>lato_users</code>, <code>lato_spaces</code>, <code>lato_cms</code>)
  adds its own permission column to the shared <code>Lato::User</code> table, and the form
  discovers them by naming convention:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><code>lato_*_admin</code> &mdash; a boolean column, rendered as a checkbox (on/off permission).</li>
  <li><code>lato_*_admin_role</code> &mdash; an integer column, rendered as a select (a role with multiple levels).</li>
</ul>

<p>
  No configuration is required in <code>lato_users</code>: install an engine that adds one of these
  columns and its control appears in the form and gets permitted on create/update. Its label is taken from
  the <code>activerecord.attributes.lato/user.&lt;column&gt;</code> translation (provided by this gem for the
  official engines, otherwise add it yourself).
</p>

<h4>Role permissions (enum)</h4>

<p>
  For a role column, the owning engine provides the selectable options (label and value) by defining a
  <code>Lato::User.&lt;column&gt;_options</code> class method that returns <code>[[label, value], ...]</code>.
  <code>lato_users</code> calls it to build the select and rejects submitted values that are not in the options. For instance, <code>lato_cms</code> exposes its
  roles through <code>Lato::User.lato_cms_admin_role_options</code>, so the CMS role appears as a dropdown
  with the roles configured by that engine.
</p>

<p>
  You can define your own role permission the same way, either from an engine or directly in the
  application. The example below adds a <code>lato_shop_admin_role</code> with three levels.
</p>

<p><strong>1. Add the integer column</strong> to the shared <code>lato_users</code> table:</p>

<pre><code># db/migrate/xxxxxxxxxxxxxx_add_shop_admin_role_to_lato_user.rb
class AddShopAdminRoleToLatoUser &lt; ActiveRecord::Migration[8.1]
  def change
    add_column :lato_users, :lato_shop_admin_role, :integer, default: 0, null: false
  end
end</code></pre>

<p>
  <strong>2. Expose the options</strong> by reopening <code>Lato::User</code> and defining
  <code>lato_shop_admin_role_options</code>. Wrap it in <code>to_prepare</code> so it survives code
  reloading in development. The method must return an array of <code>[label, value]</code> pairs, and the
  values must match the integers stored in the column:
</p>

<pre><code># config/initializers/lato_shop_roles.rb
Rails.application.config.to_prepare do
  Lato::User.class_eval do
    def self.lato_shop_admin_role_options
      [
        [I18n.t("shop.roles.none"),    0],
        [I18n.t("shop.roles.manager"), 1],
        [I18n.t("shop.roles.owner"),   2]
      ]
    end
  end
end</code></pre>

<p>
  <strong>3. Add the labels</strong> to a locale file. The option labels come from the keys used above,
  and the field label of the select comes from the <code>Lato::User</code> attribute translation:
</p>

<pre><code># config/locales/en.yml
en:
  shop:
    roles:
      none: No access
      manager: Manager
      owner: Owner
  activerecord:
    attributes:
      lato/user:
        lato_shop_admin_role: Shop role</code></pre>

<p>
  That is all. After running the migration the new role appears automatically as a select in the user
  form, gets permitted on create and update, and its value is saved on the user record. No change to
  <code>lato_users</code> is required. Reading the role back is a plain integer comparison:
</p>

<pre><code>user.lato_shop_admin_role         # =&gt; 0, 1 or 2
user.lato_shop_admin_role &gt;= 1    # true when the user is at least a manager</code></pre>

<div class="alert alert-info mt-3">
  <p class="mb-0">
    Keep <code>0</code> as the "no access" value: the form and permission checks treat any value greater
    than zero as a granted role, mirroring how boolean permissions work.
  </p>
</div>

<h3>What admins can do</h3>

<ul>
  <li>View all application users.</li>
  <li>Create new users.</li>
  <li>Edit user email, name, and available Lato admin permissions. Changing the email of another user revokes that user's sessions.</li>
  <li>Verify or revoke email verification.</li>
  <li>Send a new verification email.</li>
  <li>Set a password when creating a user, or force a new random password later. It is shown once in the response, never stored in flash or session, and all the user sessions are revoked.</li>
  <li>Delete users when allowed by the application data constraints.</li>
  <li>Create, resend and delete invitations.</li>
</ul>

<div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">
  <p class="mb-0">
    Admins cannot delete their own currently logged-in account from Lato Users.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="alert alert-info mt-3">
  <p class="mb-0">
    Users created by an admin are saved with the current privacy policy and terms versions, because
    <code>Lato::User</code> requires them. Passwords cannot be changed from the edit form. There is no audit log of admin actions.
  </p>
</div>

<h3>Recommended setup</h3>

<p>
  Create at least one administrator in seeds and enable <code>lato_users_admin</code>.
  This guarantees access to the user management panel after first deployment.
</p>

<pre><code>admin = Lato::User.find_or_create_by!(email: "admin@example.com") do |user|
  user.first_name = "Admin"
  user.last_name = "User"
  user.password = "Password1!"
  user.password_confirmation = "Password1!"
  user.email_verified_at = Time.current
end

admin.update!(lato_users_admin: true)</code></pre>

<h3>Invitations</h3>

<p>
  Use invitations when users should not self-register.
  Admins can create invitations from the Lato Users panel. Invited people receive an email and complete account creation from the invitation link.
  Accepted invitations cannot be deleted, emails already registered are rejected, and Lato limits sending to one email every 2 minutes per invitation.
  If delivery fails the invitation is kept and the panel says so: use Resend to try again.
</p>
